narrative:
You're not as quiet as you meant to be - a snapped branch, a shifted shadow, something gives you away, and the hired guard's crossbow is up and pointed at the treeline before you've fully processed being caught.

@Guard: Company! We've got company!

Whatever advantage careful scouting might have bought you is gone now, replaced with every eye at the roadblock turning your way at once.

The hostages scatter gratitude and shaken nerves in roughly equal measure. Ferrick finds you specifically before the others, uncharacteristically sincere for exactly as long as it takes to say what he means.

@Ferrick: Owe you for this one properly. Not the kind of debt I'm in the habit of paying, so treasure it.

Which leaves the matter of the Tollkeeper himself, however this particular version of him ended up - alive, disarmed, and very much still your problem to decide.

Reyes takes custody without ceremony, reading him rights and charges in the same flat voice he uses for everything. Whether that's justice or just paperwork depends entirely on who you ask.

@Bailiff Reyes: Trial, not an execution. I know what people expect from me. I try not to always give it to them.

It's the closest thing to hope you've heard from him yet.

He falls into step beside you without slowing his own pace to match, forcing you to keep up rather than the reverse.

@Bailiff Reyes: You're wondering why I'm not surprised a tax dispute turned into hostages. I'm not surprised because I watch what desperation does to people for a living, and it rarely does anything creative. Just the same handful of bad decisions, over and over, dressed up as though they're original.

There's something almost sad underneath the flat statute-quoting cadence, just for a moment.

Ferrick's composure cracks, just slightly, once the immediate danger's actually passed and there's nothing left to perform unbothered about.

@Ferrick: Don't often get reminded I'm not actually as untouchable as I like to pretend. Strange thing to owe a stranger for. Stranger still that it's you, specifically, given our own history hasn't exactly been spotless.

Whatever that history was, he seems to be reconsidering some of it, right now, in real time.

Reyes pauses before leading his prisoner off, glancing back at you with something almost like respect, filtered carefully through his usual flatness.

@Bailiff Reyes: You could have ended this several messier ways. You didn't. I notice things like that, even when I don't say so at the time.

It's not warmth, exactly. From him, it might be close.
